================================================================================
FAILURE 1: InsertProductAsync - Statement 3
================================================================================
Date/Time: 2024-12-30 11:46:59
Method: InsertProductAsync(Product product)
Statement Type: Multi-statement transaction block with INSERT, UPDATE
Conversion Attempt: DMS MCP Tool (dms-mcp____statement_conversion_tool)

Original SQL Server Statement:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DECLARE @NewProductId INT;

BEGIN TRANSACTION;
    -- Insert the new product
    INSERT INTO Products (Name, Description, Price, StockQuantity)
    VALUES (@Name, @Description, @Price, @StockQuantity);
    
    SET @NewProductId = SCOPE_IDENTITY();
    
    -- Log the insertion
    INSERT INTO ProductHistory (ProductId, Action, OldPrice, NewPrice, OldStock, NewStock, ActionDate)
    VALUES (@NewProductId, 'INSERT', NULL, @Price, NULL, @StockQuantity, GETDATE());
    
    -- Update product statistics
    UPDATE ProductStats
    SET 
        TotalProducts = TotalProducts + 1,
        AveragePrice = (AveragePrice * TotalProducts + @Price) / (TotalProducts + 1),
        LastUpdated = GETDATE()
    WHERE StatId = 1;
COMMIT;

SELECT @NewProductId;
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DMS Tool Output/Error:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
{
  "conversion_timestamp": "2025-12-30T11:46:59.687372",
  "status": "error",
  "error": "Metadata model creation failed: {'error': \"Metadata model creation failed: {'default_error_details': {'message': 'Statement definition is not valid.'}}\"}",
  "error_timestamp": "2025-12-30T11:47:14.496518"
}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Error Analysis:
- DMS tool rejected the multi-statement transaction block with DECLARE/SET
- Complex transaction structure with variable assignment not supported
- SCOPE_IDENTITY() function call requires special handling
- Multiple statements within single transaction block too complex for DMS

Manual Conversion Applied:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- This conversion requires application-level transaction management in C#

-- Step 1: Insert product with RETURNING (replaces SCOPE_IDENTITY())
INSERT INTO productmanagement_dbo.products (name, description, price, stockquantity)
VALUES (@Name, @Description, @Price, @StockQuantity)
RETURNING productid;

-- Step 2: Log the insertion (using returned productid)
INSERT INTO productmanagement_dbo.producthistory (productid, action, oldprice, newprice, oldstock, newstock, actiondate)
VALUES (@NewProductId, 'INSERT', NULL, @Price, NULL, @StockQuantity, NOW());

-- Step 3: Update product statistics
UPDATE productmanagement_dbo.productstats
SET 
    totalproducts = totalproducts + 1,
    averageprice = (averageprice * totalproducts + @Price) / (totalproducts + 1),
    lastupdated = NOW()
WHERE statid = 1;
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Manual Conversion Rationale:
1. Split complex transaction into discrete statements for C# transaction management
2. SCOPE_IDENTITY() replaced with PostgreSQL RETURNING clause
   - More efficient than LASTVAL()
   - Returns the inserted ID directly
3. GETDATE() replaced with NOW()
   - Standard PostgreSQL timestamp function
4. Schema names updated:
   - Products → productmanagement_dbo.products
   - ProductHistory → productmanagement_dbo.producthistory
   - ProductStats → productmanagement_dbo.productstats
5. Transaction management moved to C# code:
   - Use BeginTransactionAsync() on NpgsqlConnection
   - Execute statements sequentially
   - Commit or rollback based on success

Implementation Notes:
- C# code will need to capture RETURNING value
- Use ExecuteScalarAsync() for INSERT with RETURNING
- Wrap all three statements in NpgsqlTransaction
- Variable @NewProductId will be handled in C# code

